# AppArmor profile granting bubblewrap the `userns` permission.
#
# This file is shipped, not installed. Nothing in this repository copies it
# into /etc or reloads AppArmor: it needs root, and granting a binary the right
# to create user namespaces is the machine owner's decision.
#
# ============================================================================
# DO NOT INSTALL THIS AGAINST bubblewrap < 0.12.0.
# ============================================================================
#
# Read this before running anything below. Installing this profile on a machine
# whose bwrap is older than 0.12.0 makes the security posture WORSE, not better.
#
# CVE-2026-87766 / GHSA-pxhw-h44j-8pfx affects every bubblewrap below 0.12.0.
# During sandbox *setup*, creating a file or directory under the new root can
# follow a parent symlink out through /oldroot and write an attacker-chosen
# path on the host, as the user who launched it. Upstream fixed it in 0.12.0.
#
# Research OS runs project acceptance commands over a worktree a model has just
# written to. "Attacker-controlled filesystem content" is therefore this
# system's ordinary case rather than an exotic one.
#
# This profile grants bwrap the user namespace it needs. On a vulnerable bwrap,
# that converts a binary which *cannot* contain anything into one that contains
# things and can be walked out of. The current state -- containment refused --
# is strictly safer than that, which is why `researchctl runtime doctor`
# reports a vulnerable-but-working bwrap as PRESENT_BUT_UNACCEPTABLE and
# `available_backend()` declines to return it.
#
# Check first, and do not proceed unless the version is 0.12.0 or greater:
#
#   bwrap --version
#   uv run --frozen --extra runtime researchctl runtime doctor | grep -i sandbox
#
# The order is: replace the binary, then install this profile. Never the
# reverse. See docs/CONTAINMENT_OPTIONS.md for how to get a patched build.
#
# Why it is needed, once the binary is safe. Research OS refuses to run a
# model-written experiment without OS containment (`SandboxMode.REQUIRED`), and
# on this class of machine the namespace itself is refused:
#
#   $ sysctl kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns
#   kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns = 1
#
# Ubuntu 24.04 restricts unprivileged user namespaces through AppArmor. The
# blunt fix is
#
#   sudo sysctl -w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0
#
# which turns the restriction off for every binary on the machine. This profile
# is the targeted alternative: it grants the permission to bwrap alone and
# leaves the restriction in place for everything else.
#
# It follows the convention Ubuntu itself uses for this on this machine -- see
# /etc/apparmor.d/thunderbird, /etc/apparmor.d/crun and six others, all of
# which are `flags=(unconfined)` profiles whose only purpose is to name the
# binary and grant `userns`.
#
# The `profile` line below names /usr/bin/bwrap. If the patched binary lives
# somewhere else -- /usr/local/bin/bwrap, say -- change that path to match
# exactly, because an AppArmor profile binds to a path and a profile naming a
# binary that is not the one being run grants nothing. Do not point it at a
# user-writable directory: anything that can write that path would inherit the
# userns grant.
#
# To install it, deliberately, AFTER confirming bwrap >= 0.12.0:
#
#   sudo install -m 644 deploy/apparmor-bwrap /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap
#   sudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap
#
# To verify it, through the sandbox Research OS actually uses -- NOT through
# `unshare --user --map-root-user true`, which tests a different binary under a
# different profile and can pass while bwrap still fails:
#
#   uv run --frozen --extra runtime researchctl runtime doctor | grep -i sandbox
#   uv run --frozen --extra runtime pytest tests/test_sandbox.py -q
#
# The doctor line must report the backend as OK, and the sandbox suite's
# adversarial tests must stop skipping and must pass. Until those adversarial
# tests have actually run and held, `containment_validated` stays false and the
# doctor says NOT PROVEN -- a namespace is not a boundary.
#
# To undo it:
#
#   sudo rm /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap
#   sudo systemctl reload apparmor
#
# What this does NOT do. `flags=(unconfined)` means this profile confines
# nothing; it exists to lift the userns restriction for one binary. The
# containment Research OS then gets is bubblewrap's, not AppArmor's, and
# SECURITY.md states that boundary in full. It is a real OS boundary -- mount,
# PID, network and user namespaces -- and it is not a substitute for not
# running code you have reason to distrust.

abi <abi/4.0>,
include <tunables/global>

profile bwrap /usr/bin/bwrap flags=(unconfined) {
  userns,

  # Site-specific additions and overrides. See local/README for details.
  include if exists <local/bwrap>
}
